These are my notes from reading design patterns
Chap 1
- Program to an interface not an implementation
- Favor composition over inheritance
- The strategy pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangable. Strategy lets the algorithm vary indepentdently from the clients that use it.

Chap 2
- The observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes start - all of the its dependents are notified and updated automatically.
- Strive for loosely coupled designes between objects that interact

Chap 3
- Classes should be open for extension but closed for modification
- The decorator pattern adds itws own behavior either before and/or after delagating to the object it decorates to do the rest of the job.
- Decoraters provide a flexible alternative to subclassing for extending functionality.

Chap 4
- The factory method pattern defines an interface for creating an object but lets the subclass decide which class to instatiate. Factory method lets a class defer instantiation to a subclass
- The abstract factory pattern provides an interface for creatng families of related or dependent classes. 
- Factory method - define an interface for creating an object, but let the subclasses decide which class to instantaite. 

Chap 5
- The singleton pattern ensures a class has one instance and provides a a global point of access to it 

Chap 6
- The command pattern encapsulated a request as an object, thereby letting you paramaterize other objecsts with different requests, queues, or log requests, and supports undobable operations.
- Sometimes we want to return "nothing" but we don't have the client to have null errors so we can return a null object. (safe nothing)

Chap 7
- The adapter pattern converts the interface of a class into another interface that the clients expect. Adapter lets calsses work together that couldn't otherwise due to incompataible interfaces.
- The facade pattern provides a unifed interface to a set of interfaces in a subsystem. Facade defines a higher level interface taht makes the subsystem easier to use. 

Chap 8
- The template method pattern defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a method deferring some steps to subclasses. Template method lets subclasses redefine certain steps of an algorithm without changing the algorithms structure.

Chap 9
- The iterator pattern provides a way to access the elements of an aggregate object sequentially without exposing it's underlying representation. 
- The composite pattern allows you to compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole heirachies. 
- This allows clients to treat individual objects and compositions of objects uniformally.

Chap 10
- The state pattern allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes. This object will appear to change its class

Chap 11
- The proxy pattern provides a surrogate or placeholder for another object to controll access to it.

Chap 12
- The compound pattern combines two or more patterns into a solution that solves a reocurring problem or general problem.

Chap 13
- A pattern is a solution to a problem in a context
- An anti-pattern tells you how to go from a problem to a bad solution.
